You tried to run java classes generated with a 1.6 compiler on a 1.5
runtime?

This can't work this way. Java writes the version number of its
compiler into the class files and checks them when loading the files.

If you really want to compile them for a earlier JVM version you have
to tell it to the compiler:
javac -target 1.5 test.java

For even earlier versions:
javac -target 1.4 -source 1.4 test.java

If you're using language features that use byte codes or source code
features that the specified version doesn't support, the compiler will
barf.

> That's not necessarily true.  In fact, several experts in
> optimization that I know claim precisely the contrary---that you
> can get far better performance if the final optimization only
> occurs in the actual runtime environment.  Sun's JVM for Windows
> definitely does a better job of optimizing than CygWin's g++, or
> from what little I've seen, VC++.

That's an unfair comparison. The JVM with its "just in time"
compilation feature can optimize the code dynamically *while it is
running*.

OTOH Stroustroups design decision "the goddam runtime environment must
be as small as possible and that's why everything must be fixed at
compilation" prevents such a dynamic nature.

> Supposedly, the JVM specification hasn't changed since it was
> made public.  There may be problems with the standard library,
> but I doubt that they're that big.

New bytecode codes for new language features have been added between
specific versions.

